Durarara!! AU
Chapter 2 – Part 2
Curtis finally stepped back onto one of the main streets, where the neon was brighter and the crowds thicker. The constant flow of people, the chatter, the glow of shop signs—it felt almost normal after the rooftops, cracked concrete, and strange conversations in quieter corners.
He exhaled quietly behind the mask.
“Finally. No wrong turns into dark alleys where girls are jumping off roofs.”
The words were dry, almost muttered to himself as he kept walking. Hands in the front pocket of the gray hoodie, black lenses scanning the busy sidewalk ahead, he moved with the same unhurried pace as before. First night in Ikebukuro was already more eventful than he had planned.
A little further down the street, near the mouth of a covered shopping arcade, he noticed a small group of three.
They were busking.
A girl with a guitar, a guy on a portable keyboard, and another with a cajón. Their sound was clean—tight harmonies, good timing, the kind of music that should have drawn at least a small circle of listeners. Instead, people flowed past them with barely a glance. A few coins sat lonely in the open guitar case. The performers kept playing anyway, professional enough not to let the empty space in front of them kill their energy.
Curtis slowed as he approached, the music cutting clearly through the general noise of the street. He didn’t stop completely, but his head turned just enough to take them in—the effort, the quality, and the complete lack of a crowd.
For a moment he simply listened while the rest of Ikebukuro moved around them all.
The song ended on a clean final chord.
A few people walking by offered polite smiles, but no one stopped. The open guitar case still looked emptier than it should have.
Curtis clapped once—slow, deliberate, just loud enough to be heard. Then he stepped closer, reached into his pocket, and dropped a few bills into the case. Not coins. Paper. The amount was enough to make the keyboard player glance up in mild surprise.
He didn’t say anything. He simply stood there for a moment longer, black lenses shifting from the three performers to the gear they had set up beside them.
There was a second guitar resting on a stand, untouched for the entire set. Next to it, a spare microphone still on its clip, cable neatly coiled like it was waiting for someone who hadn’t shown up.
One of the band members—the guy who had been on the cajón—noticed where Curtis’s attention had settled. He wiped his hands on his jeans, gave the tall masked figure a once-over, and offered a friendly, slightly tired smile.
“You play?” he asked, nodding toward the unused guitar and mic. “We’ve got an extra if you’re interested. Crowd’s dead tonight anyway. Could use a fourth.”
The other two looked over as well, curious but not pushy. The street noise continued around them while they waited to see what the quiet stranger in the hoodie and mask would do.
Curtis didn’t answer right away.
He stood there with his hands still in the front pocket of the gray hoodie, black mask and sunglasses giving the three performers nothing to read. The street noise filled the small silence while they waited. For a moment it seemed like he might just walk off the same way he’d arrived.
Then his voice came, low and even.
“I do. I’ve got a few songs on my phone with music sheets. If you don’t mind, I can AirDrop them to you.”
The cajón player blinked, then glanced at the others. The girl with the guitar raised her eyebrows slightly. The keyboardist leaned over to look at the unused guitar still sitting on its stand like it had been waiting for exactly this kind of offer.
“AirDrop works,” the cajón player said after a second, already pulling his phone out. “Yeah. Send them over. We’re not exactly drowning in requests tonight.”
The guitarist gave a small nod, shifting her instrument to the side.
“You need the spare mic too, or you just want the guitar?” she asked. Her tone was practical, not skeptical. A dead crowd made people more open to experiments.
The keyboard player shrugged, already clearing a little space in their setup.
“Long as you can keep time and don’t mind that almost nobody’s watching… hop in. Worst case, we all look weird together.”
They waited, phones ready, while the tall masked stranger decided how much of himself he was actually going to put into the street performance.
Curtis pulled his phone from his pocket, tapped the screen a few times, and sent the files over with a soft whoosh of AirDrop. The cajón player’s phone chimed almost immediately, followed by the others. They leaned in to check the sheets, eyebrows rising as they scanned the chords and structure.
While they looked, Curtis stepped over to the unused guitar. He picked it up with the easy familiarity of someone who had done it a thousand times before, settled the strap over his shoulder, and began tuning. His fingers moved quickly and precisely, small adjustments until the instrument sat exactly the way he wanted it. The whole process took less than a minute.
The girl with the primary guitar watched him work, then gave a small, impressed sound.
“Oooh. Are you going to sing?”
Curtis glanced up from the tuning pegs, black lenses unreadable above the mask.
“I can,” he said. “But my voice isn’t the greatest.”
The three of them exchanged a quick look. The keyboard player shrugged first.
“Dude, the crowd’s already ignoring us. You can’t make it worse.”
The cajón player grinned and tapped a soft rhythm against the side of his instrument.
“Yeah. Give it a shot. Worst case, we all bomb together. Best case, somebody actually stops walking for once.”
The girl nodded, already shifting her own guitar into a supporting position.
“We’ve got the sheets. We’re good. Just start when you’re ready.”
They waited, instruments ready, while the tall masked stranger in the gray hoodie finished the last tiny adjustment on the borrowed guitar and settled his stance on the busy Ikebukuro sidewalk.
The keyboard player counted them in with a quiet “one, two…” and the little street setup came alive again.
Curtis’s fingers found the opening chords cleanly. The borrowed guitar sat comfortably against him as the cajón locked in behind and the second guitar layered support. For a few seconds it was just instrumental. Then he leaned slightly toward the spare microphone, black mask still covering the lower half of his face, and started to sing.
His earlier claim about his voice not being the greatest turned out to be a lie—or at least a severe understatement.
The tone that came out was rough around the edges but controlled, carrying both melody and weight. When the verses shifted into rapid-fire rap, his delivery stayed tight and clear even through the mask. The lyrics hit the busy sidewalk without apology:
I’m tryna be a role model for all of the kids
And keep it full throttle, fuck societies grids
So what happens if I call it quits?
Who’s on the front line takin’ all the hits?
…‘Cause money ain’t shit if you’ve got no loyalty
I love every single one of you that supported me…
People walking past began to slow.
By the time he reached the chorus, a small cluster had already stopped. Phones came out. A couple of girls near the front started nodding along. Curtis didn’t play to the growing audience. He stayed focused on the song, voice rising with the next verse about suicide attempts, lost friends, and refusing to quit:
Can’t even count the times I lost my fuckin’ mind
Pulled back from the brink, got back to my feet…
But you saved me, gave me a purpose…
This is for the ones that gave meaning to my life
This is Hollywood Undead ’til I die
The final choruses hit harder. The three original buskers locked in tighter, feeding off the sudden energy. What had been an ignored sidewalk set turned into an actual performance. The open guitar case started receiving more than spare change. People clapped on the beat. Someone in the back shouted the last “Undead!” along with him.
When the final chord rang out, the little crowd that had formed gave a real round of applause—nothing massive, but honest and loud enough to turn a few more heads further down the street.
Curtis let the guitar hang from the strap for a second, breathing evenly behind the mask. The black lenses swept once across the people who had stopped for them, then returned to the three bandmates who were staring at him like they had just been handed a completely different night than the one they started with.
The final chord rang out and the scattered applause rolled across the sidewalk. The little crowd that had formed stayed a few seconds longer than usual, some still nodding, a couple already pulling out phones to check what song it had been.
Curtis remained still for a moment with the borrowed guitar, black mask and sunglasses giving nothing away.
In the crowd, the reactions were quieter, but sharper.
Rio stood near the back, arms wrapped around herself again. She had followed the sound without meaning to. When the lyrics about the kid who got off the ledge and the pain that wasn’t worthless hit, her shoulders had gone tight. She didn’t clap. She just watched the masked man who had caught her hours earlier pour something raw into a street performance like it cost him nothing.
Celty had parked the black motorcycle a short distance away and approached on foot. The yellow helmet remained fixed on Curtis the entire time. She didn’t move with the crowd. She simply stood at the edge of it, the dark visor unreadable, processing the fact that the same man who could hear her voice had just stood in the open and sung about keeping a mask on as long as he lived.
Anri Sonohara lingered off to one side, separate from everyone else. She had been drawn by the music the same way others had. Her expression stayed soft and distant, but her eyes remained on the tall figure in the gray hoodie longer than most. Something about the controlled intensity behind the performance held her attention even after the song ended.
A little further back, Mikado Ryūgamine stood with Masaomi Kida. Mikado looked genuinely impressed, eyes bright as he leaned toward his friend.
“That was actually really good,” he said quietly. “Like… really good.”
Masaomi had his hands in his pockets, watching with a more assessing look. “Guy’s got presence. And he kept the mask on the whole time. Kinda theatrical. Wonder what his deal is.”
They hadn’t noticed Anri standing on the opposite side of the small crowd.
The Van Gang had stopped as a group near a convenience store entrance.
Kadota watched with his usual calm, arms crossed. “Not bad. Better than most of the noise we get out here.”
Walker’s eyes were practically sparkling. “The mask, the delivery, the whole tragic anti-hero vibe—he’s like a living light novel protagonist. I need to know his backstory immediately.”
Erika was already half-typing notes on her phone, muttering about “masked foreigner with tragic depth” and “potential new character route.”
Saburo just sighed, rubbing the back of his neck. “You two are already writing fanfiction in your heads, aren’t you? The guy just sang a song.”
None of them approached.
They all simply watched as the tall man in the gray hoodie, black mask, and Jumpman cap quietly handed the guitar back to the buskers and stepped out of the center of attention as if he hadn’t just pulled a crowd out of thin air.
Rio stood at the edge of the thinning crowd, arms still tight around herself.
She couldn’t stop staring at him.
The same man who had cracked the wall and caught her in mid-air was now standing on a busy sidewalk like nothing had happened, quietly returning a borrowed guitar. The lyrics kept replaying in her head whether she wanted them to or not.
So what about the kid that got off the ledge?
All the people saved…
If I could save just one kid
I’ma keep this mask on as long as I live…
It felt personal. Too personal. Like someone had taken the exact weight she had been carrying on that rooftop and put it into a song without ever asking her name. He hadn’t looked at her once during the performance. She wasn’t even sure he knew she was there. And somehow that made it worse.
He meant those words.
He actually meant them.
Her throat felt tight. Part of her wanted to walk up to him again. Another part wanted to disappear before he noticed her standing there like a ghost that hadn’t finished falling. She stayed rooted in place instead, watching the masked figure step back from the center of attention as if he hadn’t just dragged a crowd out of thin air.

A short distance away, Celty remained still.
The yellow helmet was angled toward Curtis, unmoving. Behind the dark visor her thoughts turned over slowly, carefully.
He could hear her.
That fact alone still sat heavily in her mind. No one had ever done that. Not once. Yet earlier he had answered her unspoken question without hesitation, then casually told her she didn’t need the PDA with him.
And now this.
The same man who left spiderweb cracks in concrete and refused to give his name to Izaya had just stood in the open and poured something raw and personal into a street performance while keeping his face completely hidden. The lyrics about masks, pain, and refusing to quit had not sounded like performance. They had sounded like someone speaking from experience.
What are you?
The question returned, sharper than before. Not human—or at least not only human. Strong enough to catch a falling body and walk away unharmed. Perceptive enough to hear a voice that should not exist. Controlled enough to stand in a crowd and still give nothing away.
Celty’s gloved fingers flexed once at her side.
She did not approach.
She did not type.
She simply continued to watch as the tall figure in the gray hoodie began to move away from the buskers, already blending back into the flow of Ikebukuro like the entire performance had been only a brief interruption in his night.
Whatever he was… she was no longer willing to lose track of him so easily.
A few more scattered claps and a couple of cheers followed as the last of the applause died down. The small crowd that had formed wasn’t in a hurry to fully disperse. A few people lingered, still watching the tall figure in the gray hoodie and black mask.
Curtis had already started to lift the guitar strap over his head, ready to hand the instrument back.
One of the band members—the cajón player—stepped forward quickly and caught the neck of the guitar before he could fully remove it.
“Just one more song,” he said, half-laughing, half-pleading. “That was good. Like, actually good. Crowd’s finally paying attention for once. Don’t leave us hanging after that.”
The girl with the other guitar nodded in agreement, already shifting back into position.
“Yeah. One more. You clearly know what you’re doing. We’ll follow whatever you start.”
The keyboard player gave a thumbs-up from behind his setup, clearly on board.
Curtis stood there for a moment with the guitar still in his hands, black lenses unreadable above the mask. The remaining people on the sidewalk watched with fresh interest, waiting to see whether the masked stranger would actually stay for a second song or shut it down and walk away the same way he had appeared.
Behind the dark lenses, he seemed to weigh the request in silence.
Curtis was quiet for another second.
Then he gave a small nod and settled the guitar strap back onto his shoulder.
“The file I sent over has a second song,” he said, voice even behind the mask. “We’ll do that one, if that’s okay.”
The cajón player’s face lit up immediately. He spun toward the others, already scrolling through his phone.
“Hell yeah—second track’s right here. Give me a second to pull the sheets up.”
The girl with the guitar leaned over to look at her own phone, nodding as the new charts appeared.
“Got it. Structure’s clear. We can follow.”
The keyboard player adjusted his stance and gave a short confirming nod.
“Ready when you are.”
Curtis didn’t say anything else. He simply checked the tuning one more time with a couple of quiet notes, then glanced at the three of them once. When they all signaled they were set, he turned slightly toward the remaining crowd, black mask and sunglasses still firmly in place, and waited for the count-in.
The people who had stayed—including those watching from further back—shifted with fresh attention. The masked stranger was going to play one more.
The cajón player counted them in.
Curtis started the second song with a lower, more reflective tone than the first. The borrowed guitar carried a slower, heavier rhythm at first, matching the opening lines as his voice came through the mask—steady, rough-edged, and clear:
Let me take you back, it was the start of something
I was there not long before all the stardom
Now I’m in front of you and all I’m seeing is all my flaws
I was a starving artist but I was fearless…
The small crowd that had remained leaned in. The energy was different from the first song—less explosive, more personal. Curtis kept his head slightly down at first, focused on the instrument, the gray hood still up over the Jumpman cap.
As the song built, his delivery sharpened.
Trash that mirror, let my black mask run off
Saying never better baby, am I a victim of luck?
Never better baby, at last I don’t give a fuck
Baby I’m free—
On the next rise of the melody, he shifted his weight and struck a harder chord. The motion was just enough. The heavy gray hood slipped backward off his head, catching for a second on the brim of the black Jumpman cap before falling fully down around his shoulders.
Long black braids spilled into view—two thick plaits hanging down the front of the hoodie, dark against the gray fabric. The black mask and sunglasses stayed firmly in place, but the sudden reveal of the braids changed the silhouette completely. The masked foreigner was no longer just a hooded figure. He was someone with a clear, deliberate look.
A few people in the crowd made quiet sounds of surprise.
Curtis didn’t stop or acknowledge it. He kept playing, voice rising into the countdown section as the band locked in tighter behind him.
Countdown to a hot mess, five, four, three, two, one
It’s time to live my life like it’s never been done
Shoot me like a magnet up to the sun, yeah
Better late than never—
When he hit the call, the band and the remaining crowd answered together without hesitation:
“Hell yes!”
The final stretch of the song hit harder. Curtis drove it home with the same controlled intensity as the first performance, braids shifting slightly with the movement as he leaned into the last lines. The gray hoodie now sat open at the neck and shoulders, the black mask still hiding everything below the sunglasses, the newly revealed braids framing the sides of his face.
As the final chord faded, the applause came faster and louder than before.
Curtis stood there for a moment longer, breathing evenly, the hood down, braids visible, black lenses sweeping once across the people who had stayed to listen. He still hadn’t shown his face. But the city had just seen a little more of him than it had five minutes earlier.
The applause was still going when the reactions settled in.
Rio’s eyes had gone wide the moment the hood dropped. The long black braids falling into view against the gray hoodie made the man who had caught her earlier look suddenly more real—and somehow more distant at the same time. She stared, fingers tightening in her sleeves. The lyrics about freedom and trashing the mirror sat uncomfortably close to the things he had told her on the sidewalk. She didn’t clap. She just kept watching him like she was trying to decide whether this version of him felt safer or more dangerous than the one who had saved her.
Celty remained at the edge of the crowd, completely still. The yellow helmet was locked on the newly revealed braids. Behind the dark visor her thoughts moved quickly. The strength, the ability to hear her voice, the refusal to give a name, and now this deliberate, striking appearance once the hood was gone. Everything about him felt intentional. She filed the braids away as another piece of the puzzle and made no move to approach. Not yet.
Anri stood a little apart from everyone else, hands loosely clasped in front of her. The sudden change in the performer’s silhouette had pulled her attention fully. The black mask and sunglasses still hid his face, but the long braids gave him a clearer shape—someone who chose how much of himself the world was allowed to see. She watched quietly, expression soft but focused, and did not look away even after the final chord faded.
Near the back, Mikado’s mouth had fallen slightly open.
“Whoa,” he muttered. “The braids… that actually looks really cool with the mask.”
Masaomi gave a short, impressed huff, arms still crossed. “Guy knows how to make an entrance even when he’s already on stage. First the voice, now the look. He’s not trying to blend in anymore.”
The Van Gang had a more mixed set of reactions.
Kadota simply nodded once, pragmatic as ever. “Decent second song. And he committed to the image.”
Walker looked like he had just been handed a new favorite character. “The braids. The mask. The timing of the hood drop. That’s protagonist design. Someone write this down.”
Erika was already doing exactly that, thumbs flying across her phone. “Masked foreigner with braids, tragic depth, possible anti-hero route—do you think the braids are cultural or aesthetic? Both? I need theories.”
Saburo pinched the bridge of his nose. “You’re both exhausting. He just played a song. Calm down.”
Across the small remaining crowd, the tall figure in the gray hoodie, black mask, sunglasses, and now-visible long black braids stood quietly as the applause finished, still giving none of them a clear look at whatever expression might have been under the mask.
The remaining crowd shifted their attention toward the original three buskers, cheering and tossing bills and coins into the open guitar case. The sudden success of the set had people in a generous mood. The band members were smiling, thanking people, already talking among themselves about the unexpected turn the night had taken.
In the middle of that small surge of noise and movement, Curtis moved.
He slipped the heavy gray hood back up over the Jumpman cap in one smooth motion, the long black braids disappearing from view as quickly as they had been revealed. The black mask and sunglasses stayed exactly where they were. Without a word to the band, without acknowledging the leftover applause still hanging in the air, he stepped sideways out of the center of attention and into the flow of the sidewalk.
Most people didn’t notice.
Their focus was on the open case, the smiling performers, and the residual energy of the two songs. The tall figure in the gray hoodie simply thinned into the moving crowd and kept walking, hands returning to the front pocket, pace unhurried and steady.
Only one person tracked the exit clearly.
At the edge of the thinning group, the yellow helmet turned and followed the hooded shape as it moved away down the busy street. Celty watched him go in silence, the dark visor fixed on his back until the neon and the bodies of other pedestrians began to swallow him up again.
This time, she did not lose sight of him so easily.
Celty moved the moment the crowd thickened enough to cover her approach.
She cut down a side path between two buildings, engine low, aiming to intercept the direction the gray hoodie had taken. The yellow helmet stayed fixed forward as she calculated the distance and the most likely route he would use to disappear into the denser foot traffic ahead.
When she emerged onto the next main stretch of sidewalk, the space where he should have been was empty.
No tall figure in a gray hoodie.
No black mask.
No Jumpman cap moving through the neon.
Celty slowed the motorcycle to a near stop, dark visor sweeping the street in both directions. People continued past her without interest. The masked foreigner who could hear her voice, who had cracked concrete and pulled a crowd with two songs, had vanished between one block and the next.
She sat there for several seconds longer, the engine idling quietly beneath her.
Gone.
Again.
The Headless Rider remained still in the middle of the busy night, the empty stretch of sidewalk in front of her offering no answers.